course: dictionary
lesson: 3202
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Take engagement: «The Mole had spent the day on the river, but the Rat had an engagement of long standing» with Otter. Here engagement means a promise made long ago, not a ring.
explain: Look at the words around it. Is the sentence about a couple, a party, a school, or a war? A ring points to marriage, a lesson points to taking part, and armies point to a battle. Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence?
explain: Check your choice by putting the meaning into the sentence in place of the word. If it still makes sense, you have probably found the right meaning. If not, try another meaning from your list.
fact: Engagement is a noun.
fact: Engagement can mean an agreement to marry, as in an engagement ring.
fact: Engagement can mean a formal arrangement to meet someone, as in a dinner engagement.
fact: Engagement can mean being interested and taking part, as in pupils' engagement in lessons.
fact: Engagement can also mean a battle or fight between armies.
exercise: «The Rat had an engagement of long standing with Otter.» In this sentence, what does engagement mean? | *A promise made long ago | A battle between armies | A ring worn on the finger
exercise: Priya says, «Her engagement ring sparkled in the sun.» What does engagement mean here? | A dinner at a restaurant | A fight between armies | Being interested and taking part | *An agreement to marry
exercise: Mrs Patel writes, «Sam shows great engagement in class.» What does engagement mean? | *Being interested and taking part | A meeting with a friend | A promise to marry | A battle
test: The king's army met the enemy in a long engagement at dawn. What does engagement mean? | A reminder to call a friend | *A battle between armies | Being interested in a lesson | A ring for a wedding
test: Dad says, «I'm sorry, I have a prior engagement on Friday.» What does engagement mean? | A ring for a wedding | *An arrangement to be somewhere | A comment on a school report | Taking part in a game
exam: The teacher wrote, «Lena's engagement has grown this term.» What does engagement mean here? | A promise to marry | *Being interested and taking part | A battle | A party for a couple
